The provisions of this ordinance shall not apply to:
(1) The emission of sound for the purpose of alerting people to the existence of danger;
(2) The emission of sound in the performance of emergency work;
(3) Agricultural activities;
(4) Rail and air transportation and public transportation vehicles;
(5) The emission of sound from a manufacturing, processing or assembly operation performed within an Industrial District;
(6) Church or clock carillons, bells or chimes;
(7) The emission of sound in the discharge of weapons at a firing range previously issued a permit by Glynn County;
(8) The discharge of weapons in an area zoned Forest Agricultural under the Glynn County Zoning Ordinances;
(9) Reserved.
(10) Burglar alarm when properly maintained in accordance with rules and regulations to be promulgated by the Glynn County Police Department and approved by the Board of Commissioners; or
(11) Sound volumes produced by radio, tape player, or other mechanical sound-making device or instrument from within a motor vehicle on a street or highway, which sound is controlled by the O.C.G.A. § 40-6-14. O.C.G.A. § 40-6-14 makes it unlawful for any person operating or occupying a motor vehicle on a street or highway to operate or amplify the sound produced by a mechanical sound-making device from within the motor vehicle so that the sound is plainly audible at a distance of 100 feet or more from the motor vehicle.
