(a) Mechanical Sound-making Devices Permit. Notwithstanding the provisions of this ordinance concerning mechanical sound-making devices, the Chief of Police has the discretion to grant written permission authorizing the use of mechanical sound-making devices at such a volume and in such a manner so as to create, or cause to be created, noises or sounds which are plainly audible at a distance of 1,000 feet from the source of the noise or sound between the hours of 7:00 a.m. and 11:00 p.m. Such written authorization may be given for the following:
1. Public health and safety purposes;
2. Fairs, carnivals and similar activities;
3. Parades, processions, excursions and associated festivities;
4. Outdoor concerts and theatrical performances;
5. Outdoor neighborhood functions such as lawn and pool parties, street dances and similar activities;
6. Civic and religious celebrations;
7. Recreational and athletic activities.
In determining whether to grant such permission, the Chief of Police should consider the following factors:
1. Level or volume, duration, and types of the proposed noises or sounds;
2. Source or nature of the noise or sounds (i.e. musical instruments, amplified music, etc.);
3. Level and intensity of any background noise;
4. Proximity of sleeping facilities or quiet zones (i.e. hospitals and hotels);
5. Zoning classification of the area;
6. Population density of the area;
7. Whether the noise is recurrent, intermittent, or constant; and
8. Whether the applicant or location has previously violated this ordinance.
(b) Commercial Advertising—Sound Equipment Prohibited. It shall be unlawful for any person or persons to use, operate, or permit to be used or operated any mechanical sound-making device which is cast upon the public streets or other public property for the purpose of commercial advertising or which serves to attract the attention of the public to any building, structure, or vehicle in such a manner so as to create, or cause to be created, any noises or sounds which are plainly audible from any public streets or other public property.

